# !! DO NOT TYPE A REAL VALUE INTO THIS FILE. It is committed and this repository is
# !! PUBLIC. The placeholders below are deliberately not blank, so that a value left
# !! unreplaced fails loudly at authentication instead of silently becoming an empty
# !! credential. Copy this file first; edit only the copy.
#
# TEMPLATE — committed. Copy it, do not edit it in place:
#
#   cp env.secret.stalwart-cli.example .env.secret.stalwart-cli
#   $EDITOR .env.secret.stalwart-cli          # paste the key
#   ../../../scripts/encrypt-env-files.sh apps/production/email-stalwart
#
# Run that last command YOURSELF rather than asking the agent to. See WHY THIS IS A
# TEMPLATE below — it is not a style preference, it is the whole point of the file.
#
# The copy is gitignored (`.env*` in .gitignore); only the `.encrypted` output is
# committed. Note the encrypt script globs the whole directory, so it also refreshes
# .env.secret.stalwart.encrypted — expected, not a problem.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS IS A TEMPLATE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# On 2026-08-29 the agent created the real `.env.secret.stalwart-cli` in order to leave
# placeholder comments in it. That put the path into the agent's read-set. When the key was
# pasted in, the harness reported the change *including the secret line* — even though the
# edit happened outside the agent's tools, and even though the agent had been asked not to
# look. The key was burned on arrival and had to be rotated.
#
# The fix is structural: the agent writes the template, a human copies it to the real
# filename. The agent never creates, reads, or writes the real path, so it never enters the
# read-set and cannot be surfaced by a later diff.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO GET THE KEY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. Sign in to https://admin.mail.activescott.com as **admin@willeke.com**.
#
#    It has to be the admin account. Everything stalwart-cli touches needs `sys*`
#    permissions (sysMtaDeliveryScheduleCreate, sysSieveUserScriptUpdate, ...), which a
#    regular account such as scott@willeke.com does not hold.
#
# 2. Open **Account** — the person icon at the bottom of the left sidebar, where TOTP
#    lives — then **API Keys**.
#
# 3. Create the key. Current choice: **inherit**, so it holds every permission the admin
#    account does. See SECURITY below for what that costs and the alternative.
#
# 4. **The secret is shown exactly once.** Put it in 1Password in the same moment you
#    paste it here. It cannot be read back from the server; a lost key is deleted and
#    re-created, not recovered.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# What an API key can never do, whatever its permissions: log in over IMAP, POP3,
# JMAP-mail, SMTP submission, or any CalDAV / CardDAV / WebDAV service. It cannot read,
# send, or delete a message. The server enforces that, not this file.
#
# What it can do under `inherit` on the admin account: everything an administrator can do
# through the management API, including destroying configuration objects.
#
# The alternative is `replace` mode with an explicit list and no `*Destroy` permission:
#
#   sysMtaVirtualQueue{Get,Query,Create,Update}
#   sysMtaDeliverySchedule{Get,Query,Create,Update}
#   sysSieveUserScript{Get,Query,Create,Update}
#
# That would make "the apply plans are upsert-only" a rule the server enforces instead of a
# convention the plans follow — a wrong plan or a leaked key still could not delete a
# queue, schedule, or script. Worth moving to once the workflow is proven; narrowing is a
# create-new-key-and-swap, not a migration.
#
# Rotation: create the new key, copy this template again, paste, re-encrypt, re-run the
# Job, then delete the old key in the admin UI. Nothing caches it, so no coordinated
# cutover is needed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# USED BY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The stalwart-cli Job in this namespace, as STALWART_TOKEN alongside STALWART_URL. Both
# are read directly by stalwart-cli; there is no config file.
# Design and rollout: docs/specs/stalwart-config-as-code/plan.md
#
# No quotes, no trailing spaces — everything after `=` is the value.

STALWART_TOKEN=<REPLACE-ME>
